This seems like a very fragile way to write a test. Ensure that each test is validating the behaviour of the code it is testing, rather than being a change test. Testing means 1) test fails when there is a bug, and 2) test passes when there is no bug. If regular changes made to the code require fixing tests, then the tests check nothing.

